{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014W6WajkDVoGojKnfRTePoT","type":"tool_result","content":"CLAUDE.md\nDockerfile\nLICENSE\nMakefile\nREADME.md\ncmd\ndeploy\ndocs\ngo.mod\ngo.sum\ninternal\n7af83b5 docs: refresh TODO.md (#272)\n13c30f1 lts: capture endoflife.date fixtures and the yq pin for #47 (#275)\n1c87784 ard(triage:engineer): #40 Adds four starter triage prompts (product-manager, architect, designer,… [skip ci]\n156233c ard(triage:engineer): #231 Makes the finding decoder's errors content-free. It replaces quoted val… [skip ci]\n41b7131 ard(triage:lead): #213 This is the second part of the missing-tests starter scanner. It adds t… [skip ci]\n48af6fe feat: Budgets: daily dollar caps per role and repo, part B: stat… (#270)\n5deb24a ard(triage:engineer): #204 Plans only the first of the issue's four PRs: the filing rules for LLM… [skip ci]\na253b09 ard(triage:engineer): #48 Adds a starter lint scanner role (shell script, jq normaliser and pinne… [skip ci]\n588789c ard(triage:lead): #40 Adds four starter triage prompts (product-manager, architect, designer,… [skip ci]\n1ba5310 docs: move the package layout out of CLAUDE.md into docs/layout.md (#274)\n44676a9 ard(triage:lead): #47 The request is clear and its open questions are settled: add a starter… [skip ci]\n2302a05 ard(triage:lead): #45 This issue adds a starter product-manager-review reviewer under interna… [skip ci]\nbae6a53 ard(triage:lead): #48 Adds a starter lint scanner role under internal/roles/scanners/lint (sc… [skip ci]\n9e0e040 chore: Triage approval gate posts 'ready for approval' for an is… (#263)\na72f24e ard(triage:lead): #204 This issue adds the engine that runs prompt.txt scanners as agent jobs,… [skip ci]\n0da1919 ard(triage:lead): #231 This is a small hardening fix in the scanner package. Finding-decode er… [skip ci]\n889e605 ard(triage:lead): #241 This is the first part of per-role token caps. It adds a token ledger:… [skip ci]\nb427d80 feat: Scanner: missing-tests, part 1: the Go helper (#212) (#269)\n9110e64 ard(triage:lead): #239 This is part B of the limits-aware scheduling work: config thresholds,… [skip ci]\nb3f32aa ard(triage:lead): #254 This narrows the generic-assignment rule in the log secret scan so that… [skip ci]\n4678895 Bound the gitleaks timeout when a child holds the output pipe (#198) (#199)\nf832949 feat: Mark a worker PR ready for review once every requested rev… (#194) (#197)\ndb07c00 chore: Reviewer close-out fails when its PR is merged mid-review (#184) (#196)\na27270c closeout: end-to-end plan.md merge test; keep log on conflict (#195)\n8071f91 ard(triage:engineer): #194 Adds a readiness check to the reviewer loop that marks a worker's draft… [skip ci]\n33f5067 ard(triage:lead): #194 The controller should mark a worker's draft pull request ready for revi… [skip ci]\n26a8594 feat: closeout: don't revert newer plan.md sections committed af… (#138) (#193)\n898af75 feat: Optional gitleaks pass in the log secret scan (#89) (#192)\n7951054 feat: Workers: .gitattributes changes can hide diffs from review (#142) (#191)\nf4f08f6 Refresh docs/TODO.md (#190)\n(eval):1: no matches found: --include=*.go\n18:- **Reaper** — marks dead or overdue jobs `ard:executor:failed`, keeps their evidence 24 h, then cleans up\n34:        E[\"Executors: max 3 pods, local or k8s\"]\n35:        A[\"Agent adapters: claude, kimi, dsh-tui\"]\n37:        X[\"Reaper: ard:executor:failed, keep 24 h\"]\n50:Every label starts with `ard:`, and every stage uses one grammar: `ard:<stage>:<role>` requests work, `:running` marks a claim, `:done` marks completion. A loop picks up an issue that has the request label and none of `:running`, `:done`, `ard:executor:failed`, `ard:needs-human` or `ard:hold`.\n57:    Running --> Failed: dead or overdue (ard:executor:failed)\n68:`:done` hands off to the next stage's request label, `:changes` sends the PR back to the worker, and a failed job waits until a human removes `ard:executor:failed`.\n77:| `ard:<stage>:<role>:running` | Controller on claim | An executor holds the job |\n80:| `ard:executor:failed` | Reaper or close-out | Job died or ran over time; resources kept 24 h |\n87:**One query per role per poll.** For example: `is:open label:\"ard:triage:architect\" -label:\"ard:triage:architect:running\" -label:\"ard:triage:architect:done\" -label:ard:executor:failed -label:ard:needs-human -label:ard:hold`. Six triage roles every 15 minutes, plus four reviewers, a worker and the merger every 5, is about 100 searches an hour, well under GitHub's limits.\n91:**Agents never touch labels.** The executor's close-out removes `:running`, adds `:done` (or `ard:executor:failed`), and posts the summary comment. `ard:pending` is removed when the lead finishes, so it means \"not yet triaged\" and nothing else.\n93:**Retries are explicit.** Every retry, automatic or human, is a new job with its own id (and attempt 1). A human retries a failed job by removing `ard:executor:failed`. The controller retries on its own once, only for a transient cause it can name: a rate limit, a clone failure, a controller restart (the job was orphaned, gone or never started across it), or node eviction under Kubernetes. The failed job then gets no `ard:executor:failed` and keeps its request label, so the next claim runs it again; that claim is linked to the failed job (`retry_of` in local state), so cost and causes can be traced. An automatic retry that fails again is marked failed. A usage-limit stop is not a failure: it closes out as `usage-limit`, with no `ard:executor:failed` and the request label kept, and it neither uses up nor grants the automatic retry. The re-run after the reset is a new job linked to the stopped one through `after_limit` in local state, which also carries `retry_of` over, so a limit stop of an automatic retry does not earn a second retry. Its comment says the issue is paused for the usage limit until a time. A throttle that the CLI says is \"not your usage limit\" is an ordinary rate-limit retry, not a usage-limit stop. Thresholds and early warnings are [#63](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/63).\n156:**All agent output is committed too.** Each run's full output lands in `.ard/<issue#>/logs/<stage>-<role>-<ts>.log`. Close-out redacts known secret values from the whole stream and, in the same pass, replaces the controller host's own paths with fixed placeholders: the job worktree becomes `/work`, the job home `/ard-home`, the executor root `~/.ard`, the operator's home `~` and the controller's temp dir `$TMPDIR` (skipped when it is a shared `/tmp`). Each path is also replaced in its symlink-resolved form, longest first, and only where it ends at a path boundary; bare usernames are not touched. Close-out then secret-scans the whole redacted log (the scan sees the replaced text), then caps it at 1 MiB: the first 32 KiB plus the last ~992 KiB, cut on line boundaries and joined by one truncation marker line. The full redacted log stays host-local for 7 days. Git LFS is not used (decided, #4); revisit only if `.git` growth demands it. Once the redacted copy is committed, the raw log is deleted. Triage output goes to `main` through the committer; worker and reviewer output goes on the branch and ships in the PR. **Scanner run logs stay host-local in phase 0** (`~/.ard/logs/`, 7 days) and are not committed: one run can touch many issues, and close-out redaction and the secret scan (#20, #21) land after Gate 1. What a scanner run commits is `.ard/<issue#>/issue.md` for each issue it files or joins, nothing else. Committing scanner logs, redacted, to each touched issue is a follow-up. Never committed: scanner state databases, and any log the secret scan flags. **Optional gitleaks pass (#89).** With `redact.gitleaks.enabled: true` (default false; `redact.gitleaks.path` names the pinned binary, default `gitleaks` on PATH, and is read at each close-out) the scan runs a second pass after the builtin one: `gitleaks detect --no-git --report-format json` over the redacted bytes, in a private temp dir with a scrubbed environment and a 2-minute timeout. Findings keep only the rule, line and column, never the matched text. The pass fails closed: a missing binary, an error exit, a timeout or an unreadable report means nothing is committed. A hit is treated like an internal hit: the log stays host-local for 7 days and the job gets `ard:needs-human`. `redact.gitleaks.block_autoclose_when_missing` (default false) is read by the scanner guard, which runs the same pass over findings: when set and gitleaks cannot run, scanner autoclose stops for that run too.\n204:**The stale closer is a built-in scanner.** It has no role directory and needs no job slot, executor or worktree; its settings live in the top-level `stale:` block of `config.yaml`. It is a plain controller loop that runs every 24 hours and is skipped while paused. It closes scanner issues still at `ard:pending` with no activity (issues filed under `route: none` or `needs-human` lack `ard:pending` and are never candidates; #204 may widen this) for `stale.inactive_for` (default 30 days), as not planned, with a comment. Activity means a timeline event by anyone other than the ard App. It never closes an issue that has a `:running` label, `ard:hold`, `ard:needs-human`, a linked open PR, or a human author (any author other than the App), and it closes nothing when the client is not authenticated as an App. It only comments and closes: suppression happens on the owning scanner's next reconcile, which suppresses every not-planned close for `stale.suppress_for`. A reopened issue gets a fresh comment when it goes stale again.\n218:**The cve scanner** (`scanners/cve`, a `sh` script plus one `jq` program, no build step) merges osv-scanner (all ecosystems) and govulncheck (Go reachability). Its fingerprint is `osv:<advisory>:<manifest>:<package>`, where the advisory is the smallest `GHSA-` id of the vulnerability, else the smallest `CVE-` id, else 