{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VDGuncovKsNgyWKiA2PMZL","type":"tool_result","content":"# TODO\n\nOpen issues by phase. Generated from the GitHub milestones; closed issues are removed.\n\n## Phase 0: Skeleton\n\n- #26 Gate 1: one-week dry-run soak\n\n## Phase 1: First loop\n\nNone.\n\n## Phase 2: All roles\n\n- #40 Roles: triage/product-manager, architect, designer, documentation-planner\n- #45 Role: reviewers/product-manager-review\n- #47 Scanner: lts\n- #48 Scanner: lint\n- #50 Scanner: missing-tests\n- #52 Scanner: architecture (LLM)\n- #53 Gate 3: half of scanner issues merge untouched\n- #203 Scanner: trufflehog (leaked secrets, human-routed)\n- #204 LLM scanner engine: run prompt.txt scanners as agent jobs\n- #213 Scanner: missing-tests, part 2: scan, Dockerfile and starter wiring\n- #216 Scanner: trufflehog role (#203 part 2)\n- #217 Scanner: trufflehog starter wiring and image pin (#203 part 3)\n- #221 Scanner failure streaks are visible on the status issue\n- #231 Scanner decode errors must not echo scanner output into logs\n- #254 Log secret scan: generic-assignment flags token counts and Token fields\n- #255 Conflict rounds: the worker resolves merge conflicts with main\n- #267 PR watch: check ard's open PRs for status changes every 5 minutes (configurable)\n- #268 Committer: TestBranchesStress flakes on a git worktree admin-dir race\n\n## Phase 3: Scale\n\n- #54 Kubernetes executor\n- #55 Default ard-agent image and role image builds\n- #56 Agent adapters: kimi and dsh-tui\n- #57 Budgets: daily dollar caps per role and repo\n- #58 Status issue daily digest\n- #59 Shared roles repo\n- #60 Onboard a second repository\n- #63 Limits-aware scheduling: stop starting agent jobs as the Claude subscription nears its 5-hour or weekly limit\n- #64 Token caps per role, alongside #57's dollar caps\n- #183 Version every PR merge to main as vX.Y.Z\n- #205 Reviewer aid step: run pinned deterministic tools before a reviewer agent\n- #224 Scanner: trufflehog history scan\n- #237 Budgets: daily dollar caps per role and repo, part B: status-issue notice\n- #239 Limits-aware scheduling: stop starting agent jobs as the Claude subscription nears its 5-hour or weekly limit, part B: thresholds and the one-job rule\n- #240 Limits-aware scheduling: stop starting agent jobs as the Claude subscription nears its 5-hour or weekly limit, part C: notices\n- #241 Token caps per role, alongside #57's dollar caps, part A: token ledger\n- #242 Token caps per role, alongside #57's dollar caps, part B: token check in the claim gate\n- #243 Default ard-agent image and role image builds, part 55a: local ard-agent image\n- #244 Default ard-agent image and role image builds, part 55b: default image under the local executor\n- #245 Default ard-agent image and role image builds, part 55c: role image builds for k8s\n- #246 Version every PR merge to main as vX.Y.Z, part B: version workflow (human PR)\n- #247 Version every PR merge to main as vX.Y.Z, part C: versioncheck tests, ldflags and docs\n- #248 Version every PR merge to main as vX.Y.Z, part D: owner settings checklist (human task)\n\n## Phase 4: Docs and site\nagent.go\nagent_test.go\nclaude\nerrors.go\ninternal/agent/agent_test.go:28:\tif _, err := r.Get(\"kimi\"); !errors.Is(err, agent.ErrUnknownAgent) {\n35:        A[\"Agent adapters: claude, kimi, dsh-tui\"]\n284:**Agent CLIs plug in through adapters.** Each adapter turns (prompt file, context paths, budget) into a command line and parses the exit code and, where the CLI reports them, turns and cost. For Claude Code that is `claude -p --output-format=stream-json --verbose --restricted --safe-mode --strict-mcp-config --disable-slash-commands --permission-mode=dontAsk --settings='{\"includeGitInstructions\":false,\"disableAllHooks\":true}' --tools=… --allowed-tools=… --disallowed-tools=… --model <model> --max-budget-usd <max_budget_usd> --append-system-prompt-file <role prompt> -- <message>` (`--verbose` is required by `stream-json` in print mode). Every stage (scanner, triage, worker, review) gets the same tool profile: `--tools` and `--allowed-tools` are `Read,Glob,Grep,Edit,Write,Bash`, and `--disallowed-tools` is `Bash(git commit *),Bash(git push *),Bash(git remote *),Bash(gh *)` (decided, #79). ard does not pass `--bare`: it limits the CLI to Bash, Read and Edit, and `--tools` cannot widen it (#105). The planted-config isolation `--bare` gave comes instead from `--restricted` (only managed settings and `--settings` load), `--safe-mode` (no CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md, skills, agents, hooks, MCP servers or auto memory), `--strict-mcp-config`, `--disable-slash-commands`, and `--settings` that turns off hooks and the git status snapshot (commit subjects are third-party text). `CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_BACKGROUND_TASKS=1` is set as a fixed env var (the executor allows it) so Bash cannot detach commands. The integration tests pin each of these against a worktree with planted config and must be re-run on every `TestedVersion` bump. The tool list is not a security boundary, and the deny list is advisory, because shell indirection gets around it. The real boundaries are the missing GitHub token (Docker; see the host-mode note above, #84), the committer, which commits only `.ard/<issue#>/` from a triage or review job (other edits are discarded with the worktree), and the diff guard on worker changes. An explicit tool list from the caller (`Spec.Tools`) replaces the allow list and keeps the deny list. The user message holds only fixed text and the paths of the untrusted input files. The installed CLI accepts an undocumented `--max-turns`; ard does not rely on it, so a per-job dollar cap and the executor's hard timeout bound each run. Agents run with an empty per-job CLI config dir and HOME and receive exactly one model credential, passed by name by the executor and never visible to the adapter: an API key (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`) or a subscription OAuth token (`CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN`, from `claude setup-token`); that rests on the environment, not on a CLI flag. `ard serve` chooses the credential from its own environment: `ARD_AGENT_AUTH` is `auto` (the default; the token if set, else the key), `oauth` or `apikey`, and an explicit mode never falls back to the other variable. Only one is ever passed, because the CLI prefers the API key and would silently bill it (pinned by an integration test). A missing credential does not stop startup, because deterministic scanners need none: `ard serve` logs one INFO line, and a job that needs one fails with a clear error. The executor creates the per-job config dir as `<job home>/.claude` (0700) in `prepare` (`Handle.View.ConfigDir`; under Docker it is a bind mount at `/ard-config`), refuses to start a job whose config dir is not that empty directory, and removes it with the job home; the Reaper's `KeepWorktree` retention keeps it too, and it holds no credential, since the credential travels only in the environment. `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBPROCESS_ENV_SCRUB=1`, which would keep the token out of Bash children, is not set (decided, #125). Residual risk: an API key (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`) is inherited by Bash children; on the tested release the CLI does not pass `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` to them; and either credential can still be read from the `claude` process's own environment (`/proc/$PPID/environ` on Linux, `ps eww` on macOS). The container is the boundary: it holds no GitHub token, and the credential is scoped to the model. The scrub is off because on 2.1.328 it requires bubblewrap (and socat) plus unprivileged user namespaces; Docker blocks those under ard's flags (`--cap-drop ALL`, `no-new-privileges`, default seccomp), so every Bash call would fail closed and without bwrap the CLI refuses to start. On macOS host mode it worked and matched the `dontAsk` outcomes. It can be revisited for host mode or a loosened container. What agent Bash can read differs by mode: under Docker, only the job's own credential; in host mode, far more, because any same-uid process's environment is readable (`ps eww <pid>` on macOS, `/proc/<pid>/environ` on Linux), including `ard serve`'s own, which holds both credentials. The scrub would not close that; it is part of the host-mode risk of #84. `kimi` and `dsh-tui` both run headless with `-p`; neither has a turn cap and `kimi` reports no cost, so turns and cost are optional in the adapter result. `config.yaml` names the default CLI and any per-role override, so a role can move between CLIs without touching its prompt. The Claude adapter also reads a usage limit and token usage from the stream: a limit comes from a `rate_limit_event` with status `rejected` (its `resetsAt` is the reset time), from the CLI's own error text (`You've hit your session, weekly, Opus or Sonnet limit`) and from `rate_limit` or `billing_error` errors, and it records `usage` and `modelUsage` token counts (by input, output, cache write and cache read; per model when the result has `modelUsage`, which is preferred for accounting). That text is matched only on paths the CLI itself writes (an assistant message carrying an `error`, or an errored result with HTTP 429 or `api_error`), never in agent output, because an agent can be made to print the phrase. Only the job's own agent step and its fix steps are read for a limit: a worker's test step runs agent-written code, so its output is never classified (a printed limit line there cannot block the account). With an API key a 429 stays a transient `rate_limit` retry; only subscription (OAuth) runs have usage limits. The adapter also reads `rate_limit_info.unifiedWindows` from every `rate_limit_event` into `Result.Windows` (one reading per window type, the last winning, also when the run ends with no result). The controller records the readings for OAuth steps only, from the job's own agent and fix steps and never from a test step, including killed steps with no result; it logs them as `limits.reading`. A recording error is logged as `limits.observe_failed` and never fails the job. Nothing is gated on the readings yet (#63). The committed fixtures are scrubbed real captures where a human has made them (`testdata/CAPTURE.md`) and clearly named `synthetic_*` files elsewhere.\n426:  reviewers/license-review: { agent: kimi, when: { paths: [\"**/go.mod\", \"**/go.sum\", \"**/package.json\", \"**/package-lock.json\", \"**/npm-shrinkwrap.json\"] } }\n520:- [x] **Headless modes for `kimi` and `dsh-tui`.** ([#7](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/7)) Decided: support `kimi`, defer `dsh-tui`. `kimi -p \"<prompt>\" --output-format stream-json` runs headless with stdin closed. It has no permission mode, so it runs in Docker only. Its turn cap is the `KIMI_LOOP_MAX_STEPS_PER_TURN` env var, and it reports no cost or tokens, so the adapter treats both as optional. `dsh-tui` waits for a validated engine and headless credentials. Adapter work is #56.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"30c12fcb-f052-47df-a740-77a8c382a6b4","uuid":"73a59448-dcd5-4bc7-b9fc-6045eb6f6827","timestamp":"2026-11-23T21:08:46.130Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# TODO\n\nOpen issues by phase. Generated from the GitHub milestones; closed issues are removed.\n\n## Phase 0: Skeleton\n\n- #26 Gate 1: one-week dry-run soak\n\n## Phase 1: First loop\n\nNone.\n\n## Phase 2: All roles\n\n- #40 Roles: triage/product-manager, architect, designer, documentation-planner\n- #45 Role: reviewers/product-manager-review\n- #47 Scanner: lts\n- #48 Scanner: lint\n- #50 Scanner: missing-tests\n- #52 Scanner: architecture (LLM)\n- #53 Gate 3: half of scanner issues merge untouched\n- #203 Scanner: trufflehog (leaked secrets, human-routed)\n- #204 LLM scanner engine: run prompt.txt scanners as agent jobs\n- #213 Scanner: missing-tests, part 2: scan, Dockerfile and starter wiring\n- #216 Scanner: trufflehog role (#203 part 2)\n- #217 Scanner: trufflehog starter wiring and image pin (#203 part 3)\n- #221 Scanner failure streaks are 